{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1791028930,"nonce":"tEnXfwJ/PvOiNLsnMoiQzRbgUf4=","message":"Correction to my previous comment: I was wrong about the SVG path, and about it being out of scope.\n\nI said defaulting the SVG tint to the inherited colour would turn multi-colour icons monochrome. It would not. blinc_app's render_rasterized_svgs already substitutes currentColor textually with the resolved colour before usvg parses, leaving fixed colours as written. apply_tint only fires when the document contains no currentColor at all. The raster cache already keys per colour, with a sentinel so shader-tinted variants share one texture. I had read apply_tint and stopped before the substitution branch above it.\n\nWorse, that mistake hid a regression I introduced. SvgElement's tint already fell back to props.text_color, so moving inherited colour out of props in d793a8f0 cost icons their colour: an icon inside a tinted ancestor went to its default. No test covered SVG colour inheritance, so nothing caught it. Fixed in 5cce9716, which reads the effective colour instead.\n\nSo SVG currentColor is NOT outstanding on this issue. What remains is text-decoration, which follows the colour pattern.\n\nAlso removed in 5cce9716: InheritedText.color and a slot in both carried tuples, written but no longer applied once the passes stopped copying. blinc_layout's crate-level allow(dead_code) would have kept them indefinitely.\n\nStill true from before: .color() on the SVG builder documents itself as resolving currentColor but is an alias for .tint(), which recolours every non-transparent pixel. That only bites when an author calls it explicitly on a document with no currentColor.","files":null}]}